Chapter 6
Liquidity: the Five Ratios
السيولة: النسب الخمس
Liquidity is the ability to meet obligations as they fall due. It is the first thing a short-term lender asks about, the first thing a supplier asks about, and the first group of ratios in the Learning Outcome Statements. A company can be profitable for years and fail in a month for want of cash, and every one of those failures was visible in the liquidity ratios before it happened.
Five ratios are named in the syllabus, and the exam expects all five by name: the current ratio, the quick or acid-test ratio, the cash ratio, the cash flow ratio and the net working capital ratio. They differ in one thing only — how much of the current assets they are willing to count.
Figure F506-01 is your map.
Figure F506-01. Chapter 6 roadmap: five ratios, one question, five answers.
