WHAT YOU ALREADY KNOW   What you already know
You already know what makes an asset current: the company expects to turn it into cash, sell it or use it up within one year or within the operating cycle, whichever is longer. Every ratio in this chapter is built out of that classification, which is why Part 1 spent a chapter on it.
6.1  The five ratios
All five divide something the company can pay with by something it has to pay. They differ in the numerator, and the differences are a ladder of increasing severity.
The current ratio divides total current assets by total current liabilities. It counts everything the classification allows, including inventory that has not been sold and prepayments that will never be cash at all.
The quick ratio, also called the acid-test ratio, removes inventory and prepaid expenses. What is left is cash, marketable securities and receivables: the assets that become cash without first having to be sold to a customer.
The cash ratio removes receivables too. Only cash and marketable securities count. This is the ratio for a company whose customers may not pay on time.
The cash flow ratio divides operating cash flow for the year by current liabilities at the end of it. It asks a different question from the other four: not what the company holds, but what it generates.
The net working capital ratio divides net working capital — current assets less current liabilities — by total assets. It expresses the cushion as a share of the whole company rather than as a multiple of the obligations.
Figure F506-02 sets out the definitions.
Figure F506-02. The five liquidity ratios
@table
Ratio | Numerator | Denominator | What it excludes
Current ratio | Total current assets | Total current liabilities | Nothing current
Quick (acid-test) ratio | Cash + marketable securities + receivables | Total current liabilities | Inventory and prepayments
Cash ratio | Cash + marketable securities | Total current liabilities | Receivables as well
Cash flow ratio | Operating cash flow for the year | Total current liabilities | Everything held; it measures what is generated
Net working capital ratio | Current assets − current liabilities | Total assets | Expresses the cushion against the whole company
@end
EXAM TRAP   Which assets are quick?
Quick assets are cash, marketable securities and net receivables. Inventory is never quick, however fast it sells.
Prepaid expenses are never quick, because they turn into expense and not into cash.
The cash flow ratio uses the cash flow for the YEAR against liabilities at a POINT. That mismatch is deliberate and is how the ratio is defined.
