6.3  Reading the five together
No single liquidity ratio is sufficient, and the exam sets questions in which two of the five point in different directions. When that happens, the difference between them is itself the finding.
A high current ratio with a low quick ratio means the current assets are mostly inventory. That is the distributor's position: Barada's inventory is 34 per cent of its total assets, so its current ratio flatters and its quick ratio tells the truth. A lender financing a seasonal stock build will look at the quick ratio and at how fast the stock moves, which is Chapter 12.
A high quick ratio with a low cash ratio means the quick assets are mostly receivables. That is a company whose liquidity depends on its customers paying, and the ageing schedule matters more than the ratio.
A healthy current ratio with a weak cash flow ratio means the company holds enough but is not generating. That is the most dangerous combination in this chapter, because the stock of liquid assets is finite and the flow is what replaces it.
Figure F506-05 sets out the four readings.
Figure F506-05. What the gaps between the ratios mean
@table
Pattern | What it says | What to look at next
Current high, quick low | Current assets are mostly inventory | Inventory turnover and days in inventory
Quick high, cash low | Quick assets are mostly receivables | Days sales outstanding and the ageing
Stock ratios healthy, cash flow ratio weak | The company holds but does not generate | The statement of cash flows
All five high and rising | Liquidity is ample; the question is deployment | Free cash flow and the investment plan
@end
The fourth row is Orontes. All five ratios rose together, the cash flow ratio at 1.76 confirms that the stock is being replenished by trading rather than by borrowing, and the analytical question is no longer whether the company can pay but what it should do with the surplus.
EXAM TRAP   Higher is not always better
A current ratio that keeps rising can mean uncollected receivables or unsold stock. Look at what the numerator is made of.
A very high cash ratio is idle money. The exam treats cash earning nothing as a cost, and Chapter 15 measures it as a drag on return on assets.
A ratio below 1.0 is not automatically a failure. A company with fast inventory turnover and slow payables can operate below 1.0 for years.
