6.4  The liquidity of current liabilities
The syllabus asks specifically for an understanding of the liquidity of current liabilities, and the phrase repays attention. Current liabilities are not a single block falling due at the same moment. They differ in when they must be paid, in whether they must be paid in cash at all, and in whether the creditor can demand payment early.
Figure F506-06 takes Orontes's current liabilities apart.
Figure F506-06. Orontes Foods, Inc. — current liabilities by how they will be settled
@table
Liability | Amount | How it is settled | Pressure
Accounts payable | 2,050,000 | Cash, on supplier terms | Real, and dated
Accrued liabilities | 900,000 | Cash, mostly payroll and utilities | Real, and soon
Income taxes payable | 350,000 | Cash, on a statutory date | Real and immovable
Contract liabilities | 180,000 | By delivering goods, not by paying cash | None on the bank account
Dividends payable | 400,000 | Cash, but the board set the date | Real, and within the board's control
Current portion of long-term debt | 400,000 | Cash, on the loan schedule | Real and immovable
Total current liabilities | 4,280,000 | |
@end
Two of those lines behave quite differently from the rest.
Contract liabilities of $180,000 are what Part 1 called unearned revenue. They are discharged by shipping tahini, not by paying money. A ratio that counts them as a claim on cash overstates the pressure on the bank account.
Dividends payable of $400,000 are a real obligation, but the board declared them and chose the payment date. A company under pressure can, and does, defer the next declaration. Note what Orontes did: it declared $450,000 and paid only $50,000, leaving $400,000 on the balance sheet at year end. That single decision raised current liabilities by $400,000 and pushed the current ratio down from what it would otherwise have been.
Take both out and the picture changes: 4,280,000 − 180,000 = 4,100,000 of liabilities requiring cash, and 4,100,000 − 400,000 = 3,700,000 of liabilities requiring cash on a date Orontes does not control. Against cash and securities of $7,344,100 that is a cash ratio on the stricter base of 7,344,100 ÷ 3,700,000 = 1.98.
Nobody publishes that ratio and the exam will not ask for it by name. The point is the reasoning: the denominator is a classification, and a classification can be interrogated.
EXAM TRAP   Not every current liability wants cash
Contract liabilities and unearned revenue are settled by performance.
Deferred tax is not a current liability at all; all deferred taxes are noncurrent.
Debt callable on demand is current whatever the lender's intentions, which is a rule you met in Part 1 and which cuts the other way: it adds pressure the ratio should show.
LANGUAGE FOCUS   "Current" is a classification, not a fact
English current liability is a defined classification: a label the standards attach. Arabic الالتزامات المتداولة and French passifs courants are the same. The label does not say the money leaves tomorrow; it says the obligation is expected to be settled within a year or the operating cycle. Candidates lose marks by reading the label as a statement about timing within the year.
