6.5  What a liquidity ratio will not tell you
Four limits, all examinable.
A ratio is a photograph of one day. A company can arrange its affairs so that the photograph flatters: paying down a payable just before the year end with cash it immediately re-borrows, or delaying a purchase. This is window dressing, and the clue is a ratio that moves sharply at the year end and reverts.
A ratio says nothing about the quality of the assets counted. Receivables that will not be collected and inventory that will not sell are both inside the current ratio at full value until someone writes them down. Part 1 taught you the write-down rules; Chapter 17 asks whether they have been applied.
A ratio says nothing about undrawn credit. A company with a current ratio of 1.0 and a committed and unused facility of $5,000,000 is in a stronger position than one with a ratio of 2.0 and no facility. The facility is disclosed in the notes, not in the ratio.
A ratio says nothing about the operating cycle behind it. A supermarket collects in cash and pays its suppliers in sixty days; it runs a current ratio below 1.0 permanently and is never short. A shipbuilder cannot. Chapter 12 builds the cycle that explains the difference.
Figure F506-07 lists them.
Figure F506-07. Four limits of a liquidity ratio
@table
Limit | The clue | Where it is resolved
One day only; window dressing is possible | A sharp year-end movement that reverts | Interim statements; the cash flow statement
Asset quality is assumed | A rising ratio with a rising ageing | Chapter 17, earnings quality
Undrawn credit is invisible | The notes on borrowings | The commitments note
The operating cycle is ignored | An industry that always runs below 1.0 | Chapter 12, the cash cycle
@end
