Chapter summary
Figure F506-08 brings the chapter together.
Figure F506-08. Chapter 6 summary map: the key rule for each section.
Five ratios answer one question with increasing severity. The current ratio counts all current assets; the quick ratio removes inventory and prepayments; the cash ratio removes receivables too; the cash flow ratio replaces the stock of liquid assets with the flow of operating cash; and the net working capital ratio expresses the cushion against total assets. For Orontes in 2025 they are 3.31, 2.68, 1.72, 1.76 and 0.313, every one of them far above 2024, and all five move together because one fact moved: cash rose $5,202,100. The ordering current ≥ quick ≥ cash always holds and is a check on the arithmetic. The gaps between the ratios are the finding: current high and quick low means inventory, quick high and cash low means receivables, healthy stock ratios with a weak cash flow ratio means a company holding but not generating. Current liabilities are not one block — contract liabilities are settled by delivery, dividends payable on a date the board chose — so the denominator can be interrogated. And no liquidity ratio sees past one day, past the quality of what it counts, past undrawn credit, or past the operating cycle.
Practice
Answer the multiple-choice questions first. Then try the case-style set and the written task. The answers explain why each wrong option is wrong, so read them even when you are right.
Multiple-choice questions
P6-01  The quick ratio excludes, from current assets:
A.	cash and marketable securities.
B.	inventory and prepaid expenses.
C.	receivables and inventory.
D.	nothing; it equals the current ratio.
P6-02  Use Figure F506-03. Orontes's quick ratio at December 31, 2025 is:
A.	3.31
B.	2.68
C.	1.72
D.	1.76
P6-03  Current assets are 14,188,100 and current liabilities are 4,280,000. The current ratio is:
A.	2.35
B.	3.31
C.	0.30
D.	9,908,100
P6-04  The cash flow ratio divides:
A.	cash by current liabilities.
B.	operating cash flow by current liabilities.
C.	net income by current liabilities.
D.	free cash flow by total liabilities.
P6-05  A company's quick ratio exceeds its current ratio. This shows that:
A.	the company is highly liquid.
B.	inventory is negative.
C.	an error has been made in the calculation.
D.	receivables exceed inventory.
P6-06  Which current liability is settled without paying cash?
A.	Accrued payroll
B.	Income taxes payable
C.	Contract liabilities
D.	Accounts payable
P6-07  Net working capital of 9,908,100 against total assets of 31,672,600 gives a net working capital ratio of:
A.	0.313
B.	3.31
C.	2.31
D.	0.135
P6-08  A current ratio rises because inventory is not selling. The quick ratio will:
A.	rise by the same amount.
B.	not rise, because inventory is excluded.
C.	fall below 1.0 automatically.
D.	become incalculable.
P6-09  A supermarket runs a current ratio of 0.8 for years without difficulty. The explanation is:
A.	the standards permit it.
B.	it collects in cash and pays suppliers later, so its cash cycle is negative.
C.	its inventory is overstated.
D.	it has no current liabilities.
P6-10  Orontes's cash and marketable securities of 7,344,100 against current liabilities requiring cash on dates it does not control of 3,700,000 give a ratio of:
A.	1.72
B.	1.98
C.	2.68
D.	3.31
Case-style practice: the bank reviews Barada's line
Levant Commerce Bank is reviewing Barada Wholesale's $600,000 credit line. Barada's current ratio is 2.00 and its quick ratio is 0.94. Its inventory has grown 41 per cent in a year on sales growth of 11 per cent, and its accounts payable grew 9 per cent. The bank's credit officer notes that the current ratio is "comfortably above 1.5, which is our usual threshold".
Required. Say whether the credit officer's reading is sound, giving the two figures that bear on it most. Then state what the gap between the current ratio and the quick ratio shows, and name the one further ratio from this chapter that you would want and why. Continuous prose, under 250 words.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
Answers and explanations
Section checks
SC6-1  Answer C. Inventory is in current assets and out of quick assets.
A is wrong. Cash is in both.
B is wrong. Marketable securities are in both.
D is wrong. Receivables are in both.
SC6-2  Answer B. Operating cash flow is a flow over the year; the other numerators are balances.
A is wrong. That describes the other four.
C is wrong. Net income is not used in any of the five.
D is wrong. Total assets is the denominator of the net working capital ratio.
SC6-3  Answer B. A prepayment is consumed as expense; it never becomes cash.
A is wrong. Materiality is not the reason; the rule applies whatever the amount.
C is wrong. Prepaid expenses are current.
D is wrong. They are assets of the company.
SC6-4  Answer B. The numerator grew far faster than the denominator because of the cash.
A is wrong. Current liabilities rose, from $3,570,000 to $4,280,000.
C is wrong. Inventory rose only $150,000.
D is wrong. Receivables rose, so collection did not accelerate.
SC6-5  Answer B. Each numerator is a subset of the one before it.
A is wrong. This reverses the order.
C is wrong. The quick numerator is a subset of the current numerator.
D is wrong. The ordering is a property of the definitions.
SC6-6  Answer A. A cash ratio above 1.0 means cash and securities exceed current liabilities.
B is wrong. That would be a common-size figure; cash is 23.1 per cent of assets.
C is wrong. The denominator is current liabilities, not total liabilities.
D is wrong. This is the opposite of a liquidity problem.
SC6-7  Answer C. The gap between 3.0 and 0.8 is the inventory and the prepayments.
A is wrong. Cash is in both ratios, so it cannot create the gap.
B is wrong. Receivables are in both ratios.
D is wrong. Marketable securities are in both ratios.
SC6-8  Answer B. The stock of liquid assets is finite and nothing is replacing it.
A is wrong. Holding and generating together is the healthy case.
C is wrong. Strong generation can support a low stock.
D is wrong. Both are high, which is not dangerous.
SC6-9  Answer B. Unsold stock raises the ratio and worsens the position.
A is wrong. Cash from operations is the best source of a rise.
C is wrong. Repaying short-term debt raises the ratio by shrinking both sides, which is not a bad sign.
D is wrong. A share issue adds cash.
SC6-10  Answer B. A contract liability is discharged by delivering the goods.
A is wrong. Payables are settled in cash.
C is wrong. Tax is settled in cash on a statutory date.
D is wrong. Debt is repaid in cash on a schedule.
SC6-11  Answer B. Leaving $400,000 in dividends payable raised current liabilities and so lowered the ratio.
A is wrong. A larger denominator lowers the ratio.
C is wrong. The liability is in the denominator and changed it.
D is wrong. The ratio is calculable either way.
SC6-12  Answer B. Lines differ in whether they want cash, when, and whether the date is under the company's control.
A is wrong. The standards govern classification, not analysis.
C is wrong. Deferred tax is noncurrent.
D is wrong. The current ratio includes all of them.
SC6-13  Answer B. A movement that reverts shortly after the reporting date is the signature of window dressing.
A is wrong. Seasonal trading moves the ratio and does not revert within a month.
C is wrong. An accounting change persists.
D is wrong. A misclassification would not reverse on its own.
SC6-14  Answer B. A committed facility is liquidity the ratio cannot see.
A is wrong. The ratio alone does not settle it.
C is wrong. They are not equally placed; A has a committed source.
D is wrong. An undrawn facility is not an asset.
Your turn
@table
Item | Answer
Current ratio | 2.00: 4,600,000 ÷ 2,300,000.
Quick ratio | 0.94: cash 180,000 plus securities 20,000 plus receivables 1,960,000 is 2,160,000, divided by 2,300,000.
Cash ratio | 0.09: 200,000 ÷ 2,300,000.
@end
Multiple-choice questions
P6-01  Answer B. Quick assets are cash, marketable securities and receivables.
A is wrong. Those are included.
C is wrong. Receivables are included.
D is wrong. The two ratios differ whenever there is inventory.
P6-02  Answer B. 11,488,100 ÷ 4,280,000 = 2.68.
A is wrong. 3.31 is the current ratio.
C is wrong. 1.72 is the cash ratio.
D is wrong. 1.76 is the cash flow ratio.
